WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.671
Idea. Think of a supermarket hiring shelf stackers. Workers offer their time. The supermarket wants workers. The wage is the price paid for that labour.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:31.532
On a labour market diagram, the wage rate goes up the side and the quantity of labour along the bottom. Demand for labour slopes down: firms hire more when wages are low. Supply slopes up: more people want to work when wages are high. They cross at the equilibrium wage, W e, and quantity, Q e.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:44.268
A minimum wage is the lowest wage an employer is legally allowed to pay. It only has an effect if it is set above W e. On the diagram it is a flat, horizontal line.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:55.964
Method. Step one. Draw a horizontal line at a wage clearly above W e, right across both curves. Label it W one, the minimum wage.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:09.803
Step two. Where W one meets the demand curve, draw a dotted line down. Label it Q d. Step three. Where W one meets the supply curve, draw a dotted line down. Label it Q s.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:22.577
Step four. Check. Q d must be left of Q e, and Q s must be right of Q e. The gap between them is a surplus of labour: people who want work but cannot find it.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:42.500
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2021. Using the diagram, draw the effects of a minimum wage, W one, set above the equilibrium wage, W e. Label the new quantity of labour demanded and the new quantity supplied. Three marks.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:50.886
First, find W e and Q e on the diagram you are given, where demand for labour crosses supply of labour.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:02:03.001
Draw W one as a horizontal line above W e. Where it cuts demand, drop a dotted line to Q d. Where it cuts supply, drop a dotted line to Q s.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:20.851
Why? At the higher wage, firms want fewer workers, so Q d is less than Q e. More people want to work, so Q s is more than Q e. The gap is a surplus of labour. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:32.252
Common mistake. A common mistake is drawing the minimum wage below W e. A minimum wage that changes anything must be above equilibrium.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:41.842
Another is shifting a curve, or swapping Q d and Q s. No curve moves. Demand gives the smaller quantity, supply the larger.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:03:02.396
Your turn. Now you try. An example country sets a minimum wage for fruit pickers above the equilibrium wage. Show the effect on a labour market diagram. Label W one, the quantity of labour demanded and the quantity supplied, and say what the gap shows. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:14.238
Answer. Draw W one above W e. Read down from demand to Q d, left of Q e. Read down from supply to Q s, right of Q e.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:24.047
Farms want fewer pickers at the higher wage, but more people want the job. The gap is a surplus of labour, so some pickers are unemployed.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:36.256
Remember: a horizontal line above W e, read down to Q d from demand and Q s from supply, then label everything. Now try the practice questions.
